Why addon-unicode11 is here: vt100 measures cell widths with the
`unicode-width` crate, and the TUI is full of emoji and box drawing.
xterm's default width tables disagree on several of those, which would
shift every column after one. The addon brings xterm to Unicode 11, which
matches.

Why addon-fit is NOT here: it resizes a terminal to fit its container.
Every agent-mux session shares one pane size owned by the desktop TUI, and
the remote must never resize a PTY, so the page scales the element with CSS
instead. Leaving the addon out removes the temptation.

To refresh, re-run from this directory and update the hashes above:

  curl -sSo xterm.js           https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xterm/xterm@5.5.0/lib/xterm.js
  curl -sSo xterm.css          https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xterm/xterm@5.5.0/css/xterm.css
  curl -sSo LICENSE-xterm.txt  https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xterm/xterm@5.5.0/LICENSE
  curl -sSo addon-unicode11.js https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xterm/addon-unicode11@0.8.0/lib/addon-unicode11.js
